Therefore df -h doesn't show it.

=> Why isn't df -h showing them?

Because lsblk and df -h show different things.

Think:

lsblk
  ↓
"What disks/devices do I have?"

df -h
  ↓
"What filesystems are currently mounted?"

=> So what do we want?

We want Linux to remember:

"Whenever this server starts, automatically mount this EBS volume at /data-ebs."

That's called a persistent mount.

We configure this using:

/etc/fstabServer starts
     ↓
Linux reads /etc/fstab
     ↓
Finds the EBS volume
     ↓
Mounts it
     ↓
/data-ebs is available

So think of /etc/fstab as a to-do list for Linux when it boots.

Step 1: I've run these commands so that it will explain exactly which disk is which and we'll set up /etc/fstab properly so that after the next reboot:

/data-ebs   → automatically mounted
/data-lvm   → automatically mounted

