sudo pvs
sudo vgs
sudo lvs

Explaination:- /dev/nvme1n1  12G ──┐
                   ├── datavg → datalv 20G
/dev/nvme4n1  10G ──┘

pvs confirms:

PV           VG      PSize     PFree
nvme1n1      datavg   <12G       0
nvme4n1      datavg   <10G       1.99G

So:

Total VG = 21.99G
LV       = 20G
Free     = 1.99G

This is exactly what we expected.

Step 2: /dev/mapper/datavg-datalv is your LVM filesystem

blkid says:

/dev/mapper/datavg-datalv
UUID="b6562be5-bb19-490f-990b-100d307d8ca6"
TYPE="ext4"

So your LVM logical volume has an ext4 filesystem, but currently it is not mounted.

That's why df -h didn't show it.

Step 3: Now the interesting part: your two 20G disks

You have:

/dev/nvme2n1  UUID="eb97baf4..." TYPE="ext4"
/dev/nvme3n1  UUID="eb97baf4..." TYPE="ext4"

Both have the same filesystem UUID.

This strongly indicates they are the original and snapshot-restored copies of the same filesystem.

That's why we should NOT put both into /etc/fstab using their UUID.

If both are mounted using the same UUID, you'll have a conflict.

